---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_TITLE, RELATIONS, pageHref } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title={`Method · ${SITE_NAME}`}>
  <header class="wrap site-head">
    <a class="site-name" href={pageHref()}>{SITE_NAME}</a>
    <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Site">
      <a href={pageHref()}>All movements</a>
      <a href={pageHref('about')}>About</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Method</h1>
      <h2>What counts as a movement</h2>
      <p>A movement is mapped here when it passes three tests. It is a whole view of AI: what AI is, where it is heading and what to do about it. It was born in or against the tech world, and its people use its name, with founding texts, a vocabulary and institutions.</p>
      <p>Two movements in one family differ in what they value or what they fear. A strand shares its movement's demand and fears but may ground its values differently. Effective accelerationism, rationalism and effective altruism were first mapped as movements; they are now strands of techno-optimism, Pause and AI safety.</p>
      <h2>Six families</h2>
      <p>The twelve movements are grouped in six families, each named for what it wants done: transcend, accelerate, prevent, protect, reclaim and doubt. The families run from the largest claims about what AI will become to the smallest. Each movement sits next to the one it shares most people with.</p>
      <p>Protect shields people living now; Prevent guards humanity against a machine that may come. Doubt is not a home for critics: it holds only those whose whole view is that AI is ordinary or oversold.</p>
      <h2>A movement page</h2>
      <p>Every movement page is built the same way. It opens with what the movement holds today and who holds it now, then reads it through four lenses. Then come its people, the words it uses and its own history, newest first, back to its oldest documented root.</p>
      <h2>The four lenses</h2>
      <p><strong>Values</strong> and <strong>Fears</strong> are the movement's own, named in terms shared across the map, so one movement's value can be seen as another's fear. <strong>Promises</strong> gives the order of society it aims at and the older tradition it echoes.</p>
      <p>Under Promises, the line that begins “If its demands became law tomorrow” is the site's own reasoning, not a quote. It follows the movement's own demands one step only: the first consequences, as the movement's own logic would predict them.</p>
      <p><strong>Interests</strong> starts with the funding: public records only, dated, with amounts where public, from organisations and named public funders, never private giving. Grants to a movement's organisations are kept apart from investment in companies. Where no funding was found, the page says so.</p>
      <p>Then, after “Its demands could serve”, the site's reasoning again: who stands to gain, who would lose, and the opponents who could gain anyway. It names interest, never motive: a group could see a benefit, but it is never said to be behind a movement. Each benefit comes with the mechanism that produces it.</p>
      <p>Funders are named only in the funding part, so the page never says that a funder gains. The same test applies to all twelve movements.</p>
      <p>This is how the site critiques without critics. Each movement is taken at its word and followed one step. The reader sees two gaps: between what it promises and what would happen first, and between whom it claims to serve and who could gain.</p>
      <h2>The history of AI</h2>
      <p>The history of AI is the ground all twelve movements stand on: the machines, ideas, laboratories, money and laws. It runs newest first, in eras, back to the oldest documented root. Each moment is tagged with the movements it shaped and the key words it belongs to.</p>
      <p>One story is one moment: when a later event continues an earlier one, it is told in the same moment, in order. A text enters only once it became an event: acted on by governments, labs or the public, or still shaping the debate's words. Other manifestos belong on their movement's own history.</p>
      <h2>Key words and the vocabulary</h2>
      <p>Below the movements are the words the debate keeps returning to. Every word is in the vocabulary, with what it means, who coined it and when, and which movements use it. A few are key words, shown first, each with a page that compares how the movements see it.</p>
      <p>Criticism is not a separate section. On a movement page it comes from the lenses, not from quoted critics; a key-word page still says who disputes the word.</p>
      <h2>Themes</h2>
      <p>The themes are lines of investigation into AI as it is deployed, not movements or words. Each follows one argument step by step to the point where it turns, and ends on an open question. They are drafts: none is yet researched or checked.</p>
      <h2>People</h2>
      <p>A person is on the People page when the site names them for what they did. They are among a movement's people, in a moment of the history, or the coiner of a word. Public roles and documented actions only; no private individuals and no minors.</p>
      <p>What the page shows about each person is worked out from those entries, which are checked. Roles, labs, money, departures and connections are added only once a second reader has checked each against its source.</p>
      <p>The selection leans towards the United States and Britain, where much of this debate has been loudest. It will widen to China, continental Europe, Africa, India and Latin America.</p>
      <h2>How roots are marked</h2>
      <p>Every root in a movement's history says how it is connected to the movement today. The most important difference is between ancestors the movement claims for itself and ancestors that historians attribute to it.</p>
      <ul>
        {Object.values(RELATIONS).map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Every entry names a real, specific source, and links to it where possible. Quotations are short, at most one from each source; everything else is paraphrased.</p>
      <p>Texts are first drafted, then checked claim by claim against their sources by a second reader. Entries not yet checked are marked as drafts.</p>
      <h2>Images</h2>
      <p>All images are made by people, never generated by AI. Each names its source and its rights. Images whose rights are not yet cleared are shown as labelled placeholders.</p>
      <p>The cards for movements and key words are collages of archive fragments: objects, machines, drawings and engravings. AI is itself a kind of collage, made from human knowledge, and it hides its seams. These collages show theirs: fragments cut along a grid, out of register, each with its year and source.</p>
      <p>Portraits appear with the people, on the People page and on each movement's page. They are shown in black and white, so that paintings and photographs sit together, and each credits its photographer or artist and its source.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
